</user_query>"}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Below is a **step-by-step recipe** you can copy-paste into PowerShell to clone the official proto files, copy just the parts TF-Serving needs into your Android module, regenerate stubs, and re-build.

After that I list the **Cloud Run checks** to guarantee gRPC traffic is accepted.

---

## 1 · Clone the proto sources to Desktop

```powershell
# 1-A : TensorFlow Serving APIs
cd "$Env:USERPROFILE\Desktop"
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/tensorflow/serving.git

# 1-B : TensorFlow Core framework protos (dependency)
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/tensorflow/tensorflow.git
```

(Fresh clone ≈30 MB each with `--depth 1`.)

---

## 2 · Copy ONLY the proto files we need into your project 

From the project root (`C:\Users\Mohammed\AndroidStudioProjects\ObjectDetector`):

```powershell
# 2-A : Create proto folder inside the app module
mkdir -p app\src\main\proto

# 2-B : Copy Serving API protos
Copy-Item "$Env:USERPROFILE\Desktop\serving\tensorflow_serving\apis\*.proto" `
 app\src\main\proto\

# 2-C : Copy TensorFlow-core framework protos that gRPC stubs need
mkdir -p app\src\main\proto\tensorflow\core\framework
Copy-Item "$Env:USERPROFILE\Desktop\tensorflow\tensorflow\core\framework\*.proto" `
 app\src\main\proto\tensorflow\core\framework\

# 2-D : Optional – copy tensor_shape / types protos in protobuf folder
mkdir -p app\src\main\proto\tensorflow\core\protobuf
Copy-Item "$Env:USERPROFILE\Desktop\tensorflow\tensorflow\core\protobuf\*.proto" `
 app\src\main\proto\tensorflow\core\protobuf\
```

Now `app/src/main/proto` contains:
```
tensorflow_serving/apis/*.proto
tensorflow/core/framework/*.proto
tensorflow/core/protobuf/*.proto
```

---

## 3 · Add protobuf + gRPC gradle config

(`app/build.gradle.kts`)

```kotlin
plugins {
 // existing plugins …
 id("com.google.protobuf") version "0.9.4"
}

dependencies {
 // existing deps …

 implementation("io.grpc:grpc-okhttp:1.60.0")
 implementation("io.grpc:grpc-protobuf-lite:1.60.0")
 implementation("io.grpc:grpc-stub:1.60.0")
 implementation("com.google.protobuf:protobuf-javalite:3.25.1")
}

protobuf {
 protoc {
 artifact = "com.google.protobuf:protoc:3.25.1"
 }
 plugins {
 create("grpc") {
 artifact = "io.grpc:protoc-gen-grpc-java:1.60.0"
 }
 create("grpckt") {
 artifact = "io.grpc:protoc-gen-grpc-kotlin:1.4.1:jdk8@jar"
 }
 }
 generateProtoTasks {
 all().forEach { task ->
 task.plugins {
 create("grpc")
 create("grpckt")
 }
 task.builtins {
 create("java") { option("lite") }
 create("kotlin") { option("lite") }
 }
 }
 }
}
```

---

## 4 · Generate stubs & build

```powershell
# from project root
$Env:JAVA_HOME = "C:\Program Files\Android\Android Studio\jbr"
./gradlew clean generateDebugProto assembleDebug
```

You should now have:

```
app/build/generated/source/proto/
 debug/java/ ← Java lite classes
 debug/kotlin/ ← gRPC-Kotlin stubs
```

---

## 5 · Use the generated stub in your client

```kotlin
private val channel = ManagedChannelBuilder
 .forAddress("mobilenet-ssd-grpc-879557471105.europe-southwest1.run.app", 443)
 .useTransportSecurity()
 .build()

private val stub = PredictionServiceGrpcKt.PredictionServiceCoroutineStub(channel)

suspend fun predict(pixelData: Array<Array<Array<Int>>>): GrpcPredictionResponse {
 // build TensorProto with official builder
 val tensor = buildTensorProto(pixelData)
 val request = predictRequest(tensor)
 val response = stub.predict(request)
 return convert(response)
}
```

*(building helpers omitted for brevity – now you can use the real generated
`TensorProto`, `PredictRequest`, `ModelSpec`, etc.)*

---

## 6 · What to verify on Google Cloud Run

| Check | Setting |
|-------|---------|
| **Container listens on** | **`PORT=8080`** (default for Cloud Run) **AND** the container must start **gRPC server** on that port. |
| **Protocol** | In Cloud Run console → *Container, Networking & Security* → **“Ingress protocol” = HTTP/2 (gRPC)** |
| **Deploy flag** | If you used `gcloud run deploy`, ensure **`--use-http2`** (or `--port 8080` with gRPC server that speaks HTTP/2) |
| **Health checks** | Disable HTTP-only health checks or make them point to a simple “/” that returns 200; Cloud Run sends HTTP/1.1 health pings. |
| **Authentication** | If Cloud Run service is **private**, you need an Identity-Aware Proxy token; easiest during dev is set service to **Allow unauthenticated**. |
| **Domain** | Ensure the URL you call ends with the Cloud Run hostname **without path**; your gRPC stub adds the `/tensorflow.serving.PredictionService/Predict` pseudo-path automatically. (**Do NOT** append `/v1/models/...` when using pure gRPC.) |

---

## 7 · Re-deploy and retest

```bash
gcloud run deploy mobilenet-ssd-grpc \
 --source . \
 --use-http2 \
 --platform managed \
 --allow-unauthenticated \
 --region europe-southwest1
```

Once redeployed, run the app again. 
A successful gRPC call will return a `PredictResponse` with your detections.

**That’s it!** 
Proto files are in place, real gRPC stubs are generated, client code uses them, and Cloud Run is configured to accept gRPC."}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<user_query>
