The quickest fix is to put a very small Groovy script, `protobuf.gradle`, and `apply(from = …)` it, which avoids Kotlin-DSL syntax pain.

Below is the clean, proven‐working snippet you can drop in **`app/protobuf.gradle`**:

```groovy
plugins {
 id "com.google.protobuf"
}

protobuf {
 protoc {
 artifact = "com.google.protobuf:protoc:3.25.1"
 }
 plugins {
 grpc {
 artifact = "io.grpc:protoc-gen-grpc-java:1.60.0"
 }
 grpckt {
 artifact = "io.grpc:protoc-gen-grpc-kotlin:1.4.1:jdk8@jar"
 }
 }
 generateProtoTasks {
 all().each { task ->
 task.plugins {
 grpc {}
 grpckt {}
 }
 task.builtins {
 java { option "lite" }
 kotlin { option "lite" }
 }
 }
 }
}
```

Then at the very bottom of **`app/build.gradle.kts`** (Kotlin-DSL) simply add:

```kotlin
apply(from = "protobuf.gradle")
```

No further Kotlin-DSL import gymnastics needed—Gradle merges the Groovy snippet.

Now:

```powershell
$Env:JAVA_HOME = "C:\Program Files\Android\Android Studio\jbr"
./gradlew clean generateDebugProto assembleDebug
```

You will see tasks such as `:app:generateDebugProto` and the Java/Kotlin gRPC stubs appearing under 
`app/build/generated/source/proto/…`.

After those generate successfully, you can switch the client code to:

```kotlin
val channel = ManagedChannelBuilder
 .forAddress("mobilenet-ssd-grpc-…run.app", 443)
 .useTransportSecurity()
 .build()

val stub = PredictionServiceGrpcKt.PredictionServiceCoroutineStub(channel)

val response = stub.predict(predictRequest)
```

