<previous_assistant_message>
No — what you have now is ​not​ a legitimate TensorFlow-Serving gRPC client, so a 502 is expected.

Why it’s wrong
• We’re still sending an arbitrary binary blob over an ordinary HTTP request (`POST …/v1/models/…`). 
• TF-Serving’s real gRPC service lives at the root path (`/tensorflow.serving.PredictionService/Predict`) and expects the full gRPC framing (length-prefixed “gRPC frames”, headers `content-type: application/grpc`, trailer `grpc-status`, etc.). 
• OkHttp alone can’t do that; you need the gRPC Java library (`io.grpc:*`) plus the generated stub classes from the official proto files.

What “correct” looks like

```kotlin
val channel = ManagedChannelBuilder
 .forTarget("mobilenet-ssd-grpc-…run.app:443")
 .useTransportSecurity() // TLS
 .build()

val stub = PredictionServiceGrpc.newBlockingStub(channel)

val tensor = TensorProto.newBuilder()
 .setDtype(DtType.DT_FLOAT)
 .setTensorShape(
 TensorShapeProto.newBuilder()
 .addDim(Dim.newBuilder().setSize(1)) // batch
 .addDim(Dim.newBuilder().setSize(300)) // height
 .addDim(Dim.newBuilder().setSize(300)) // width
 .addDim(Dim.newBuilder().setSize(3)) // channels
 )
 .addAllFloatVal(pixelFloats) // 270 000 floats
 .build()

val request = PredictRequest.newBuilder()
 .setModelSpec(ModelSpec.newBuilder()
 .setName("mobilenet_ssd")
 .setSignatureName("serving_default"))
 .putInputs("input_tensor", tensor)
 .build()

val response: PredictResponse = stub.predict(request)
```

What you must do

1. Copy official proto files 
 `tensorflow_serving/apis/*.proto` 
 `tensorflow/core/framework/*.proto`

2. Apply `com.google.protobuf` Gradle plugin and generate Java classes + gRPC stubs.

3. Add dependencies 
```gradle
implementation("io.grpc:grpc-okhttp:1.60.0")
implementation("io.grpc:grpc-stub:1.60.0")
implementation("io.grpc:grpc-protobuf-lite:1.60.0")
```

4. Create the real stub (`PredictionServiceGrpcKt` or Java stub) and call `stub.predict(...)`.

5. Use the Cloud Run URL ​without​ “/v1/models/…” (Cloud Run must be deployed with `--use-grpc` so port 443 speaks gRPC).

Until you do those steps, the call will never match TF-Serving’s expectations, and 502/415/404 errors are normal.

So: revert the hand-rolled code, generate the official stubs, and use the true gRPC channel + stub. That is the only TF-Serving-compliant solution.
</previous_assistant_message>

