At this point the only robust solution is the **standard layout**:

```
app/
 └─ src/main/proto/
 ├─ tensorflow_serving/apis/…
 ├─ tensorflow/core/framework/…
 ├─ tensorflow/core/example/…
 ├─ tsl/protobuf/…
 └─ xla/tsl/protobuf/…
```

and tell Gradle’s protobuf plug-in to treat all nested directories as valid
proto roots—without moving files.

Working configuration
---------------------

In `app/protobuf.gradle` (Groovy script already applied from
`build.gradle.kts`):

```groovy
apply plugin: 'com.google.protobuf'

protobuf {
 protoc { artifact = "com.google.protobuf:protoc:3.25.1" }

 // ←--- This is the only part we need.
 sourceSets {
 main {
 proto {
 // just the *top* folder; plugin recurses automatically
 srcDir 'src/main/proto'
 include '**/*.proto'
 }
 }
 }

 generateProtoTasks {
 all().each { task ->
 task.builtins {
 java { option 'lite' }
 }
 // uncomment after core compiles
 // task.plugins { grpc {} }
 }
 }
}
```

Important points 
• `include '**/*.proto'` *inside* the `proto` block is valid in **Groovy**. 
• Keep *one* `srcDir` (`src/main/proto`) and let `include` recurse; do **not**
 add separate `srcDir` lines for every sub-folder. 
• Don’t copy files upwards; preserve directory structure exactly like in the
 TensorFlow repo.

